    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Moscow was a minor border town when it was first recorded in 1147, and it rose under Daniel's descendants, who collected tribute for the khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and drew the seat of the Russian Church to the city.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> united nearly all the Russian states with Moscow and rebuilt the Kremlin, and in 1547 Ivan the Terrible was crowned there as the first tsar of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> moved his government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1712.</li><li>Moscow became the capital again in 1918 under Lenin, grew through the Soviet period, held back the German army in 1941 and 1942, and remained the capital of the Russian Federation after 1991.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1937 letters were written to the Central Committee of the Communist Party proposing that Moscow be renamed &quot;Stalindar&quot; or &quot;Stalinodar&quot;. Stalin rejected the suggestion.</p></aside>
    </section>

